<table role="presentation"><tbody><tr><td><div style="height:20px;line-height:20px"> </div></td></tr></tbody></table><!--[if mso | IE]><tr><td class="" width="600px" ><table align="center" border="0" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" class="" role="presentation" style="width:600px;" width="600" ><tr><td style="line-height:0px;font-size:0px;mso-line-height-rule:exactly;"><![endif]--><div style="margin:0px auto;max-width:600px;"><table align="center" border="0" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" role="presentation" style="width:100%;"><tbody><tr><td style="border:1px solid #DCDCDC;direction:ltr;font-size:0px;padding:0;padding-top:0px;text-align:center;"><!--[if mso | IE]><table role="presentation" border="0" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"><tr><td class="" style="vertical-align:top;width:598px;" ><![endif]--><div class="mj-column-per-100 mj-outlook-group-fix" style="font-size:0px;text-align:left;direction:ltr;display:inline-block;vertical-align:top;width:100%;"><table border="0" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" role="presentation" width="100%"><tbody><tr><td style="vertical-align:top;padding:0;"><table border="0" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" role="presentation" width="100%"><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="background:#FFFFFF;font-size:0px;padding:16px 16px 0;word-break:break-word;"><table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" width="100%" border="0" style="color:#000000;font-family:Georgia, serif;font-size:13px;line-height:22px;table-layout:auto;width:100%;border:none;"><tr><td valign="top"><span style="font-size:24px;font-style:italic;font-family:Georgia, serif;font-weight:400;line-height:140%;color:#052962">Jordan Brown, deputy editor</span></td><td width="110" height="90" style="line-height:0;font-size:0"><img src="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9assuTJHoR_xtxJtNZ-864UmcytBrLi1edGBYt9wCOrO3TJIayVcOtuqYBgkaKCGVnB08BIs3oUlqPyb5Bj0RW4qxUzhdbbCG8smjypao2dT6OE8FuDmsk9VaSes_NlZ4IJFh1WtsspwQ2onZ0EoW0i0tKNeP2LJBK6hfQvwLMavuiEv7dMCzD0sQuSNlvuvI1A/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t3/h001.F45uAhoIjVN81lXyZ5f7V6heHUv78lHvoSY6-LoGLhc alt="Jordan Brown, deputy editor" width="110" height="90"></td></tr></table></td></tr><tr><td style="border-top:2px dashed;border-top-color:#999999;background-color:#FFFFFF;padding-bottom:8px"> </td></tr><tr><td align="left" class="text" style="background:#FFFFFF;font-size:0px;padding:0 16px;word-break:break-word;"><div style="font-family:Georgia, serif;font-size:16px;font-weight:400;line-height:22px;text-align:left;color:#121212;"><p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Three years ago, I joined the queue for the tube at Wembley with my family and hordes of other dazed but elated England fans who had witnessed <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBMZeDECeoAWTgDwiLNHCZ3uxfxaKsTBK9WDrk4fTtJdd5In1a4cFuNKB_YoyyfytBTQlzihg9Cn5O9jppioyNWFEjBg61oNGZtJIAPwfRL92VzfRCNta_dpETs9DIBJQIqu60SeVU9KfJHdy4T-QOUUWLYcCt0eYeE0C-ow2Qtor5wSBuihjp7zMdrGG0_deCG-0G6rZwcyJDOxawCDwXOjkjFzu5B-9wdskywbMJZ4qhdhrcJ_YSFbrYj221Pkz7rbRdudvOUBxsyzmQRrj3UIiZstls5vgE0IS2TcP7J5qg/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t4/h001.4qyrUuGMM8uIqvqdLygARmwrOMn7jqHYzNcxpPAwHQs style="color: #0077B6;">England’s historic victory</a> in the final of the 2022 Women’s European Championships. For England fans conditioned to – at best – glorious failure, it was a strange but thrilling feeling. But it also marked the start of a new era: with women’s football not just back page but front-page news (as the picture of England’s Millie Bright, above,<strong> </strong>proves). At the Guardian, we had for a long time overindexed on covering women’s football, but the last three years have taken that to a new level.</p><p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0;">This week the 2025 edition of the Euros began in Switzerland with interest at a renewed high. It’s an exciting moment for anyone invested in the game, as our coverage this week in the run up to kick-off proved.</p><p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0;">It included the arrival of US national team manager Emma Hayes <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBMZeDECeoAWTgDwiLNHCZ3utWlb40990Jy_-3ox27QX01-RzyzvCAWu7zMBDbAXlmzvNUSiq5vdTB4dWNJFA0qkXuJitWuQ_zY-CxhplEudns-w3osi2_YApHa3GEwQxUkv45kGWL4RyhA_wqLNmcSywq2ygHSt9mOWxXTO_BiQ8qSs7b0jxCP1zDagzNpygYVXRwDVHLjso9lUZ-ZJ1xVdPrh-nRLyy6Hw1PvOfaKVn-suTX1GnptCGGVEevBCWZm9Y-22UTqcuR4uWemLN6p2jJN-7KiVy-9twc62u1JheWVQpwTSYHxUbL6_IhdbKGI/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t5/h001.bAofn1m0-7vZcuBQPIikmzTACqdx1susQ8E2-mcN33I style="color: #0077B6;">as a columnist</a>, women’s football correspondents Suzanne Wrack and Tom Garry’s <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBPU_enmS-YAxzxUKRnRq3qs5fvqORVpYG_BdIKHll_a1Au_5TLUEQTkXyg9QTRRKVV5SbiJB4U4lv0D-0SVlHMGEOnwsC1hs45EQSwxUuuU0XNeZbzCJ0Yg74bI41zytcvJxnAM2-lWD9E2tsX1wCJUfItyxU5TbyhOTJmdpDH1HumHXstZfrrk8P97BJIX7FXU0IosxCbdcBHZUe2TsIhCnLDiWZlP04F-p4M2dHaLSPH3WVvqs2G4Si1WSDNCmtNW4-EKtdjDPkUDcFD2qDK1zd_qI-n604mg1M5oTbbncw/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t6/h001.k5sZ-fHbK9hyPeN3OFU0q_mOU4jtpcwxQYhLBKWWDTE style="color: #0077B6;">oral history of</a> England’s great day in 2022, Spanish football correspondent Sid Lowe’s interview with Barcelona <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBMZeDECeoAWTgDwiLNHCZ3uQaUOFilpIcV32cRaC6sWueomgWQ97S6SdYRUl6D8zRXY2112mx8obtNWBcRI7vw7ER_uhePMOmLSRqihzXmF00XPq3KeWfgY-vetHInc7T4UvZjOc6np_wBOoiArCpVl_pFYu_3zl9zRQejwLjqudSy-B3kO2DiDcXlBcZAESTnt1fLpj2dVfjF18EfMsph38DTU-HgxVmgPtuttMUCDx4-YQKIrw7D56A8dOHcTSs1-xjG0qdhx4_Drp1EEEuwN/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t7/h001.QIF14Hd-xtgdLSdxqjI3IcAzHtpQRffI3OjYJ7qqgfI style="color: #0077B6;">superstar Alexia Putellas</a> and Louise Taylor’s with <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBMZeDECeoAWTgDwiLNHCZ3uzdOjnpb32djvc2_PzhBl0jATj0esmPunTV1IzA7ylxhg5lAeEpv8KEQ5-U0Hco6lpUsa_qnkliHssn7UNwxb-9XMHm_HF_mU6sQGYrM2Xi6zdiyuQCg6PBYft5BlQ6MJkYInwvyKOyZbqeBb8dHaeOq27xh9k7ZNDJEK7okwpfX5OtWjSaYRmMhu9-XyaH-v1yhuDD-oQG2ULLSlaEPrn129p8TzrOD278qAL2AshikZeWT64K9qV6X0hecCrRo9HBOFiDRXbl3IhK9ybREa1-GwtVjZQE8dLsmu-zfVmPnTPXRe_q97D4DugycZ_uaB/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t8/h001.Dafy4fxfqkSoQKNSBVmqqkTHoEk3m_-8NZZgbPf8atw style="color: #0077B6;">Jess Fishlock</a>, Wales’s greatest female player. Our hilarious football cartoonist David Squires also produced a delightful Swiss-themed wallchart, which is <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBOb5dlYUlbyGiKcsNWNh9O_uh1cQYZ1H67Qrf4bV99N82OYcahZgprMNJszoNAZ_lggu6CJYKvO2Xy42iRayNlcl5CNF6AyauaeBV107n7kKSt020e_gFNckWaUagWMvNua3qT1hQm7JNqclWE7GyO6g_nNg-krE1IeoUxwF9-VUO6tR0KW3pfle1fcwGSBjAo21-Tc0piOvt5--WSRzf64A40M6sQyMjVVjiLIVs2gXPGaMilPS_ta5VFs0h04DURLRrP2j7xKtI7gZtPlshxD5AycgWdpWrR3irl0rm3DGg/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t9/h001.8eFoACMdvkNeF1z04fa7tHm0zdWzOBS-zfJRBC9E7SU style="color: #0077B6;">available as a printout online</a> and our writers delivered their <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBMZeDECeoAWTgDwiLNHCZ3uCKEidYRlfS61Q0Q9CVNGgUoCgnKYHrcsyn-M2xRlqF1Feq8KXGzPU7AED_-iZcxL0g6WUOSYmWlFt-wYFAeIEO59JE4TwyFLnNtyHoe-zlVbV0U5SI4uigCoDsq06hBIPRETG8FyiVfdyGDwjdnAIODibodTonqa6DDCctsBVhId87sUpspz2js9nGt3z4tt04uXYqMmSLr-zIhp2sd6XCnusvwTqx-qrv5TXKe0xdljoU-7SPfQbiG9iQwYDgcLNkvSdU6TnOBQfXvEVHraNg/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t10/h001.WiuxVP1l10Rx7d_sOejyp8lwq1ks1ZjoJFuZjlzu95Q style="color: #0077B6;">pre-tournament predictions</a>.</p><p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0;">As the tournament goes on, the Guardian will be providing you with unrivalled coverage of all 16 teams (if you’re keen to know more about any player, check out this interactive guide <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBPU_enmS-YAxzxUKRnRq3qs5fvqORVpYG_BdIKHll_a1Cr-KJRS1DCxhYLJi-UDyGPHZo7ogTTSpDRtFkRItO4uAiuk6Nt4JxaV7fNQVHNLVKUbCA9IF7zCMY-1DZ6jf9GNkEVMs7lcuSM1XQOYZNMgShHphu2f28-gc52ncnRdeax1hQ4FdLcMIKAXqNLmO7q8EikA0m_dO8D5V2TcFl7itajbxOrMig5md93YrRmdUMLJoz1pq_DbXoVxI-EYQWUKwr18MviWhpluAZ1j017MBe1P0qqwwoRIteMr92S1Lw/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t11/h001.kJUM5UrsU0TgihTRgxP60OmF-hnn6YgRYHYGguXJGy4 style="color: #0077B6;">to all 368 who are at the tournament</a>) – we’ll have live blogs and match reports of every game, plus more interviews, the <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBMa0xaL0m0fYFX6aH0_Zt79EVknZ6NUmb1AKIKf5byxTsU5QgmEAv5b_aK96IN1UKph1lygTSB3M0hoyZSSBBFFa14pKs_L8f4myaHD2HA9ZsuHaIhoAl04pioOd5cd3jGeKhj7Q4_M1ACHDfaD555E5VJYEwBsv5kIpd8X55c8sG-ALL7S0oO7GcUgzWvYLGK7WWD-HcGoC-79FPTuVvOauF0pDEmWb2gbdJWUi7PylA/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t12/h001.C-qSRt_0cr--SXZGbT_VnXGZhHhA_OBkyyhfDGrN_E0 style="color: #0077B6;">Moving the Goalposts newsletter</a>, the <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBMa0xaL0m0fYFX6aH0_Zt79MKTrnxL_6-oc9xA3wlTVjCZWP8BXtlsskRnz3RXKW6HjXq7El1HvrYvIahoVEXV98WQLtqe8nGrC99XfaC_qC7tlr7MlECU_UWxboyQoP1XQOSSPp7MHgt4XS4BC9X2JOUzRXby0mV5iB0FG7m6_sJwYjys5IgnzpJdD0dXR2o4gZ6-9TXCJ4HDWOaGpg97rdebTb0bHTUiIbAY8IUA5wqdBMhXlna2Nlq864DFn8G0/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t13/h001.wmnL5i_EB-NipVonXUttEYCyTbWUgPHPIMdPMQe-SS0 style="color: #0077B6;">Women’s Football Weekly</a> podcast and much more.</p><p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0;">As some other media organisations have scaled back their coverage of women’s football, we’ve redoubled our efforts and been rewarded with ever growing global audiences including a generation of girls (such as my now 13-year-old daughter) inspired by the Lionesses to become devoted players and fans.</p><p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0;">As it expands, with some of the inevitable accompanying growing pains, the need for the women’s game to be scrutinised and held to account as well as celebrated is also not a task that our correspondents will shirk. For the next few weeks though, it will be all about the action on the pitch in Switzerland.</p><p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0;">More broadly, we’re in the midst of a huge few weeks of sport. Our team in London is <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBO2QX6nLFYwaQzS4z54QKukKSSM375MOdMOcH9lHgXoNvWvFsYSi-AISihEvd3tcVi_Wmvb6LSxcYeaf4syOkwoFp7bhdLGYmnp8ZsXrnAWVFk3A4G80Prmeah102Sfxbkb-SZIa9WdJBQmEUnmTGukcvdwL094B8h6s-yGNxvP90NXBdOEpBNTtis_6LW9tOLVfwprEaZmFKizIFUsBU2l/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t14/h001.HxNWnLS_tsJGkxTzUxiCZI5OFVpGec9vspgxJVb6QAc style="color: #0077B6;">covering all the big moments</a> as Wimbledon moves towards its final rounds, the <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBOieyNlhFgfqXPUFXjf-a0HiUQzSgnQMBBMDG2gCo9aUxZYtFAaJtCH_iMxZzXvy20dDVwUkYg4thx6mX1Cm7eFJ9uAQ7GkT9F0D_vSbuHtLFOf_rGeqB88LXLr--Pdi654kr7wdHwQJK47H_TNZ8m40cM7Ch2rv3ir15Z3YzM_sbT4eVn3TpnA-L1-BkCa-_jt6SmBVWvplbBmbWX_lUGRjleTskqVCLNCJ4JpFkVVIQ/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t15/h001.RXilNc7Wfrq84OsOTCh2T6xFULz14AQQ9rKXWBlYfFg style="color: #0077B6;">Tour de France</a> grand départs from Lille this morning (we’ll have every stage of that on our live blog too) and, of course, Fifa’s expanded <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBNxBpeLIEC-l43_jxmGy6LmxrKcIuexU_iDJDRRo0DPFj8nZV__7Hupev8F_IluiHAi-6itwL3taukKP3C750r9qCi0PzvmWNONiDbA0TwHGfbTFOtDK3yui7h0JRb_lkzuPtSMj4xP0Zl4hEcH6kNb2g_HGS5nxtMFTTjL31CyHD4iLK0RGS9d1sKAZ3OcKCBa9mh7oFJIKAyFm5CJHdeWvJjlRUi8nwACXU1hQP46kg/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t16/h001.8QIQs3EOANJMzm58-sHrsMQ_qcWJR39KnLXQ0uUy95Y style="color: #0077B6;">Club World Cup</a> is lumbering towards its lucrative conclusion amid sweltering heat and half-empty stadiums in the United States. Sadly, there was some awful sporting news this week,