01.wLnW4KuieZfhiI9bfI45avrilDVmx5iufaVFF8Liflg style="color: #0077B6;">harrowing testimony from witnesses and survivors</a>. Jason went on to reveal how evidence suggested that a <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBMVnXklOxnRgNaWIiQcKjqgcgJcFNsCUwKMswa9y10kfWBunNrxPDQ6-0JShER7xl0YLde_7-vbbAzBULSDMzEb5D0pIJeRfY0E59vQKQb5FVaGoaPeTmlzvc4ri-BeDWOoi5psMYo-YMKttpxPerh8Ur10ZPFprRuzsiBPUAYxEH5m8BY8NQnjmNXbRlqwugbk5401ppQBqIvnsSMWUuOHPZaC3CL9BUzVfS1A3MqKb3Q8g8siYgp0dwVyHFZ-kAEBkPmnycqkPZYIGi5Y2stl/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t36/h001.cmbeaXCctAcdz0knE087WeJrBKlsb5BKnBgKINdZb8k style="color: #0077B6;">500lb bomb was used in the strike</a>, the use of which could constitute a war crime according to experts. And in the aftermath of <strong>the crisis over Iran</strong>, Deepa Parent and Will Christou reported on a <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBMVnXklOxnRgNaWIiQcKjqgFpGGzX8h0M4hWWJMIBBfOTerR2SqbqoxOdPy8zlR7OGTJT5eq5ocHuCW0qyyYkh5xBfpoPSXdzQSoJsodAsmXqqta6h8rysiCAC1YNj539m2o8lVhD5DxhrvHTuTRwDlV8SN7ZsH9d7fB_pozgPsOYcfpu-fls___EDB1-nByPG32HyeQztxHDrvmfSKWZYRUfhpc9F0T-6VO5tIyxOAa7cyHqH2hxxWo2xsVvGdqmzsuy9meuIcIi7vLCYqgT50/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t37/h001.I-JO_WxXdOMFiNnOD0dEoafJi9tX0vor0v8n5i8R-SM style="color: #0077B6;">new wave of repression of political prisoners</a> as the Iranian regime attempts to reconsolidate its control.</p><p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0;">As a <strong>punishing heatwave</strong> <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBM3m8r9z1xmycoesx2ZCgQqtifCRr-HjaE-c1cgpuHEfVyh_uANhvSlZs30ysIUhvEBsIsAEPCtjnetKGtoYX-NfTxioZs3zNRTg-o1HfXZk0-CSCT5sFuI4QLc0PD5-AYz_qZuPMpW_QjOIil6UNku4qjuodMg_lKEFF1V8u31ZjlKkctBE2r2_p2h-CHbNs6R3ZQBBH3U4bMSuCPLbHetaj-upTprQgFUR1LzTDGb1lWlgRuArxO-oB_Dr4mA-z6cieFYNtEoGsTNsf4U7S5V9_uZoZXdus62hvuwNjmcleS-tBv7jh8uD7IpS3RENSY9oOBvAX3Rj0dwwnKoAZTp/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t38/h001.vsi2zlaT97ZqS5nbDFMMBIzx6kGb-X1iERDg9lfPu4k style="color: #0077B6;">engulfed much of Europe</a>, our network of correspondents reported on temperatures that, in several cases, broke records for June. In the southern Spanish province of Huelva on Saturday it was 46C. As Teresa Ribera, the EU’s green transition chief, <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBMVnXklOxnRgNaWIiQcKjqgZWck2q-o5gleEXoOIA71NTlLV_K64GN73j6e9V7vpU7_E_RF4A2rOBuld9AoUorjBJEtOKggDGeUlwuBd3B98pDcCkQJ5GVkyjCVb5AP0ZEiVsrAIoi7Gc4BSE3E1Ms4a_1WZxx-9K-H6WWNFZZqwBFVEzRhg-bVkXjpi7e5ZlWZhnOjlgY1RBVKEq-1xhyN5x0N8VW804bqX9OLnfgtfXPqn7BUOilL2LDtIh_yW8661SCZChvPnXMibuVqujQLO_-jA66kmHf3zm7CvHdJaefoIy0Zs6IOEPikgRv_IHo/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t39/h001.36b6IWm2W4yZimKlEewJqB4euO2-Y5ovnGfohJEgv8k style="color: #0077B6;">told our reporter Sam Jones</a>: “That is not normal.” European environment correspondent Ajit Niranjan wrote a <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBM3m8r9z1xmycoesx2ZCgQqSbr2w7564C7z3BEUvTRvdW9DFvk7Qnk7IfZMplwaHUjo5lMp8rLcIlTjEv124ik6R3e84JkuBeoBQjrIFPx8SF8-Ck2sN959uwSPQ06750SemwqenquinHIpVOOAYXnTmlm6h51wbHe_969c-6_iMADMhuync9BLsDNHnMjYwamVq6zx9bFY1e-AjMq2qM9cdbN5pDd1XkneJnIQ5epRrO9cMA7h8fvIF9-JmKGUW-g3y8d3YvKRBdHu93Agva-cOQTWViXsPhbRgcun8AK7VQ/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t40/h001.w2oPjyvqUH2dd7mRwsNEti5_gWGUb7wHoHCYfQMFiMY style="color: #0077B6;">helpful explainer</a> on why heatwaves are getting worse (just as EU policymakers are <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBM3m8r9z1xmycoesx2ZCgQq2HlPDOF_3bxxn-4PCMP1LFi_gkye9et3iO1jakgF61hKx6tkHGwto2n64ZQ2o5RFAvnAkh86317tmbNE1Q5vZM_Vb2j9rQ1B5iiwb6YKomvG7eHsSnVbFY8fvoeT5biLx4SFOCYUYJrrJzW9keuk-I4gzu9dC7lRdOt-BvbOBo2rs3QHJBpsQfoo9pdAv88XcqjRAt9gvhgBAb7nIuyV5_3Ik0O-soRlvigU9PysO6PIiqIjHkryXXriWU8WG1L8AqyyDXI6x6eVDpBHekppfifNuXE-N9QTAGXZR9BkIQ0/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t41/h001.i9xn5gDaXqkyO_Rd0NJvDn8L9ZEHNqjyq0fPEkr1Ww0 style="color: #0077B6;">rolling back green policies</a>). In the US, Nina Lakhani wrote <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBO2xZEcDhZvi30zK70agsnuPBSMI9W-Kqfo0-dd-JquP_HIf8H3b9JRQFT-iUNUSnPs0AEE97-h_XXELrXrZJJmkXPjW9MnTCQKQa1Tox_M3OxIo4lq6ENTpPJNiWEgqpEa9py8185lgeTjSEpg0VzezL7RdEPiTzQ9ayNsyiLnAYeHj3CekYzdiHmYPEDjKGweC2sZJQMb--DOA4HYk65GqBF8N7mjznfJkWZ6pA4gSImuXw2OdAKGrLIK4PXUQ7ndlKuDKeLaSEiNBrrFebjl/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t42/h001.c7tgSsBoV0RLH-bnaQdrdkTaXPSoyeGH0LxqDFHzcAU style="color: #0077B6;">a deeply reported piece</a> warning of a lost generation of scientists in the US, as funding cuts and political interference drives talent overseas, while, in Australia, as meteorologists were warning of a “bomb cyclone”, Petra Stock looked at the <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBM3m8r9z1xmycoesx2ZCgQqecxoPbEcSlJf4d69SphdO196LkIN8zOgDV9bRcbVW5tMDkS842NgEQZEnNZVLoUD0nTtnF04KfAd_H5qV2Ok6-qNcrFZzwQOyh3uOlWIf_-Yb-HrRH1UG59GGgBJeWxYodex1nfFS_uqdgTag5pJKuA9e09pL9howCxYSuof4PCaGrfkeDg7iTGAcrWO6AipbYig5TLMnWm7K6IOshiGSPdNEDAEkk8JH5BUjAQtRTDlG4zN73xlhcSSrCNu2CxDvgzUGnaP1oTgF3PQmlskClNfiDnKNAn4wGvIOsrMCgk-fvcoBY3fyPEsMuSGRcf5/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t43/h001.dfaeXVPdtz9l15zMSMn4D6Vo6KsgG5ECbfNaF0B4UM4 style="color: #0077B6;">importance of the language we use</a> when talking about the weather.<br><br>It’s been an astonishing week in <strong>UK politics</strong>, with the Labour government’s last-minute decision to scrap plans to cut disability benefits culminating in chancellor Rachel Reeves crying in the House of Commons. Jessica Elgot was the first to report that <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBNbj7zdiqcpvZICBrfPJUtn-qpx4w8h8NU7dYkeB2yQxh_uKD_gxqRlgLLZI-kCYkHsxPD-lejlwjlrjZNuMZvBkuLzcSr77