001._tA4mY5A_NbeaI2dzQ19sVaBcqKL56xYorJzBHp0QTU style="color: #0077B6;">chose their favourite tune</a>.</p><p style="margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><strong>One more thing: </strong>Three years ago <a href="https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.-maoMBGKBrIn6te6KzY9apUCjm_UVImAPea-qhHewBOYFiNiVNzDBb0vdi-8M3LoeywyBN2CuXpjmCgo6vmdM_OlPM7nr5qwXg7Qd8ZQJpZcdRqDGwXr1Ex9GrNgwQcag7d02KzUFUxR1KWe-DHK_e3Mwtpo9yKZlo7y0pr0E_AKnIgQ4Zl0ZJAjExbPOdQNSz_LMLl_kgGe3VXB52hsI2Zi0oYZZ1bVm-8b-gOU92MASUrAUNK1LitVq7UzD5ZYojuJ3KykKlJQKjUUur5Ioc5Oac7dNvNLNUvQhbN6DfpNuH4Z9ambeeICrV5n3xkEdEwLS5T--OP3pVOj2XUpK-Fs-fcjZ5ix6P_yHVd8oK8/4hx/C6woAoPbRqilX5KfMvsU_g/t75/h001.yqYNmSD9d9sraEotCJMiGFZaDEgqggPhT3lZGHNQRDE style="color: #0077B6;">Antony Szmierek</a> was a teacher in Manchester. But the likable pop poet has enjoyed a vertiginous rise since with his slice-of-life vignettes musing on everything from the Great Pyramid of Stockport to the symbolism of a piece of confetti tumbling from the ceiling and the fate of the subject of a message graffitied on the M60. His joyous Glastonbury set is still available on iPlayer in the UK and his album, the Service Station at the End of the Universe, is a welcome reminder of life’s simple pleasures.</p></div></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr></tbody></table></div><!--[if mso | IE]></td></tr></table><![endif]--></td></tr></tbody></table></div><!--[if mso | IE]></td></tr></table></td></tr><![endif]--> <!-- Braze Placeholder - Above Section 4 --><table role="presentation"><tbody><tr><td><div style="height:20px;line-height:20px"> </div></td></tr></tbody></table><!--[if mso | IE]><tr><td class="" width="600px" ><table align="center" border="0" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" class="" role="presentation" style="width:600px;" width="600" ><tr><td style="line-height:0px;font-size:0px;mso-line-height-rule:exactly;"><![endif]--><div style="margin:0px auto;max-width:600px;"><table align="center" border="0" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" role="presentation" style="wid